# The fragment graph

A project snapshot tells us which text belongs to one interpretation. The fragment
graph describes how its program pieces depend on one another.

Each fragment family is a node. Each reference is a directed edge from the family
that contains it to the family it names. Output roots identify the nodes from which
tangling begins.

For a graph `G = (V, E)`, `V` denotes the fragment families and `E` the references.
If the project has `|V|` families and `|E|` references, a complete traversal should
grow in proportion to `|V| + |E|`, apart from the text copied into outputs. In
plain language: checking one more fragment or one more reference should add a
bounded amount of graph work.

## Families collect ordered contributions

A family may have a definition followed by append contributions. The family is one
node even when its source appears in several documents. Each contribution retains
its own source location, language, and encounter order.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Family[Fragment family: validate a request]
    First[Definition in one chapter] --> Family
    Second[Append contribution in a later chapter] --> Family
    Third[Append contribution in another document] --> Family
    Family --> Combined[Ordered fragment content]
```

This distinction matters for diagnostics and navigation. A generated statement may
belong to the second contribution even though the family was first defined
elsewhere.

## Reachability selects output content

Tangling starts at a root and visits every referenced family. A family that is not
reachable from that root contributes no text to that output. It may still be used
by another root.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    RootA[Root A] --> Shared[Shared family]
    RootA --> OnlyA[Family used only by A]
    RootB[Root B] --> Shared
    RootB --> OnlyB[Family used only by B]
```

Shared families make reuse explicit. They also make context important: the same
source may be expanded more than once, possibly with different surrounding
indentation. Read-only editor services can select a deterministic occurrence;
mutating services need stricter rules because one source edit affects every use.

## Missing edges and cycles

A missing reference names no family. A cycle returns to a family already active on
the current expansion path. Neither problem can be repaired by emitting a partial
file.

Depth-first traversal makes both failures easy to identify. Keep a set of families
on the active path. When following an edge:

1. reject it if its target does not exist;
2. report a cycle if the target is already active;
3. otherwise visit the target;
4. remove it from the active path when that visit finishes.

A completed-family cache avoids repeating validation for shared subgraphs. The
active-path set and completed cache have different meanings: one detects recursion
on this route, while the other records work already proved valid.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Unseen
    Unseen --> Active: begin visit
    Active --> Invalid: missing target or active target
    Active --> Complete: all children complete
    Complete --> Complete: reused by another reference
    Invalid --> [*]
    Complete --> [*]
```

## Bounds protect the explanation and the machine

A valid graph can still be impractically deep or expansive. Literate places limits
on nesting, fragment segments, and intermediate text. Crossing a limit rejects the
plan rather than truncating the program.

This is a semantic choice. Silent truncation could produce syntactically valid but
incorrect source, which is more dangerous than a visible failure. A bound must
therefore be described as part of the accepted project model, not as an optimization
that may alter results.

## Determinism supports more than generation

Stable graph order underpins:

- reproducible source and HTML;
- fixed-point self-hosting;
- fragment definitions and references in the editor;
- generated-to-source mappings;
- predictable diagnostics;
- publication identities and indexes.

Determinism is therefore not merely a convenience for tests. It is the condition
that lets several tools discuss the same project without inventing separate models.

The graph gives us valid structure, but not yet a complete parsed project. The next
chapter explains how Literate collects source, Markdown tokens, contributions, and
publication data without performing generation or filesystem effects.
